## Purpose / Description

I use this workflow to automatically turn newly uploaded patient questionnaire PDFs into organized Excel records. When a PDF is added to a monitored Microsoft OneDrive folder, I retrieve the file, extract its text, identify which questionnaire type it belongs to, use an Anthropic Claude chat model to read the document, and capture the patient details, consultation information, provider, record ID, and questionnaire answers in a structured format.

The workflow is designed to support multiple mental health and clinical questionnaire formats, including PHQ, GAD, MADRS, DSM-5, Y-BOCS, Post-Concussion, TBI Symptom Pre-Treatment, and Urge to Use Scale forms. After extraction, I match the questionnaire to the correct worksheet in a Microsoft Excel workbook, check whether the patient response already exists, and then append the clean data into the appropriate sheet. If a matching worksheet does not already exist, the workflow can create a new sheet with the right column structure before saving the response.

The key technologies used are Microsoft OneDrive for file intake, PDF text extraction for reading uploaded forms, Anthropic Claude for intelligent questionnaire understanding, structured output parsing to keep responses consistent, and Microsoft Excel for storing results in a usable spreadsheet format. The workflow also includes validation steps to reduce duplicate entries and keep data aligned with the correct questionnaire columns.

This automation can be adapted to many similar document-processing scenarios. For example, the same structure could be used for intake forms, insurance forms, school assessments, HR forms, compliance checklists, or any recurring PDF document where important fields need to be extracted and logged into a spreadsheet or database. To adapt it, I would change the monitored folder, update the extraction instructions for the new document type, and point the final output to the desired reporting or storage destination.